Subject: GTK on Phoenix: the paint watch, where each web-process frame spends its time in the UI

WebKitGTK 3 shows a page differently from WPE (shared patch 0020's UI half watches WPE's): the UI
process imports each dma-buf frame of the web process as an EGLImage-backed GL texture and draws
it into GDK's own GL-composited window. Per frame: AcceleratedBackingStore::frame() takes it (no
rendering fence on Phoenix, patch 0016, so the fence monitor queues a redraw of the view at
once); GDK's frame clock paints the window at its next cycle; paint() makes the frame current
(swapBuffersIfNeeded(): the texture on a buffer's first use, the previous frame released) and
draws it with gdk_cairo_draw_from_gl(), then sends FrameDone; GDK draws the other widgets,
uploads what cairo drew, and calls eglSwapBuffers(). The web process composites its next frame
only after FrameDone, so every one of those steps is on the frame's critical path.

This records each of them, on the main thread (where all of them run), for webkit-browser
--present-stats, which forwards the toplevel's frame clock (before-paint, after-paint) and asks
for a report:

  webkit_gtk_phoenix_paint_watch_before_paint()
  webkit_gtk_phoenix_paint_watch_after_paint(line, size)  1 + "n= wait= before= swap= draw= after=
                                                            rx_to_after= dt=" when a frame was drawn
  webkit_gtk_phoenix_paint_watch_report(line, size)       counts (received, drawn, superseded,
                                                            dropped, frame_done, repaints, ...) and
                                                            <name>_ms=<avg>/<max> since the last one

It also keeps shared patch 0020's UI-process counts (frames received, drawn, frame-done sent; the
pending age), so wpe_phoenix_frame_watch(1, ...) describes the GTK UI process as it does WPE's,
and logs once what GDK will do with the texture:

  WPEB-WEBKIT gtk-paint import pid=<p> size=<w>x<h> fourcc=AB24 modifier=0x... alpha_bits=<n>

(with alpha, gdk_cairo_draw_from_gl() first uploads the cairo surface under the view as a
texture of its own, every paint, then blends the frame over it). OS(PHOENIX) only; the cost is
a few clock_gettime() calls per frame.

